2026-11-19 11:21:24,742 - test - INFO - Existing record: {'id': UUID('01a1f1cd-b47b-7f0a-acc0-552309f8d32f'), 'title': 'The Black Parade Comes to Malaysia', 'link': 'https://businesstoday.com.my/2026/11/17/the-black-parade-comes-to-malaysia', 'snippet': 'Reach Publishing Sdn Bhd 15-3, Block F1, Jalan PJU 1/42, Dataran Prima, 47301, Petaling Jaya, Selangor\nMalaysians can now join the broken, beaten and the damned, as MCR are set to bring The Black Parade 2026 tour to the TM National Stadium in Bukit Jalil November 19th (tomorrow!) and 20th.\nIn a recent social media post, Rapid KL said the final boarding time at Bukit Jalil LRT station on the Sri Petaling Line is 12.30am for direct services towards both Putra Heights and Sentul Timur. Passengers requiring transfers must leave the stadium earlier to catch connecting trains. Those connecting to the LRT Ampang Line must board at Bukit Jalil by midnight to catch the final connecting service departing Chan Sow Lin at 12.13am. Teenagers, I mean, concertgoers relying on the LRT Kelana Jaya Line, KL Monorail or MRT Kajang Line must board at Bukit Jalil by 11.30pm.\nTo ease traffic congestion around Bukit Jalil, Rapid KL urged concertgoers to use its Park and Ride facilities linked to the LRT Ampang, LRT Sri Petaling and MRT Putrajaya lines.\nKelana Jaya Line passengers can transfer at Masjid Jamek to catch the final train to Putra Heights at 12.03am or Gombak at 12.17am.', 'published_at': datetime.datetime(2026, 11, 18, 3, 15, 52, 497087, tzinfo=zoneinfo.ZoneInfo(key='Etc/UTC')), 'source': 'businesstoday.com.my', 'article_language': None, 'article_type': 'news', 'content': 'Malaysians can now join the broken, beaten and the damned, as MCR are set to bring The Black Parade 2026 tour to the TM National Stadium in Bukit Jalil November 19th (tomorrow!) and 20th. We’re not okay (we promise).\nTheir Southeast Asian tour was originally scheduled for April and May this year but was postponed, with the band citing logistical considerations and the need to ensure the smooth execution of the regional leg.\nThe shows will mark their first performances in Malaysia in nearly 19 years, with the band once again bringing songs from their third studio album and magnum opus, The Black Parade, to local audiences.\nFormed in New Jersey, US, in 2001, MCR comprise frontman Gerard Way (vocals), Mikey Way (bass), Ray Toro (lead guitar), Frank Iero (rhythm guitar) and Bob Bryan (drums).\nThe band emerged with a distinctive blend of punk energy and emotionally charged songwriting that resonated with audiences worldwide, which earned them the title of the pioneer emo band.\nTheir mainstream breakthrough came with their second studio album, Three Cheers for Sweet Revenge, which includes hits such as I’m Not Okay (I Promise), The Ghost of You and Helena.\nHowever, it was their landmark third album, The Black Parade, released in 2006, that cemented their legacy.\nThe concept album merged glam-rock spectacle with emo intensity through tracks such as Welcome to the Black Parade, Famous Last Words, I Don’t Love You, and Teenagers.\nAfter releasing their fourth and most recent studio album, Danger Days: The True Lives of the Fabulous Killjoys in 2010, the band went on hiatus in 2013 before reuniting in 2019, returning to live performances after a six-year break.\nSince their reunion, MCR have continued to attract large audiences, particularly with their Long Live: The Black Parade stadium tour, which began in 2025.\nThe tour’s 2025 North American leg grossed approximately US$88.6 million (RM362 million) across 11 reported performances, selling over 448,000 tickets, according to box-office figures compiled by Touring Data.\nThe band have since extended the tour into 2026, taking their The Black Parade production to audiences across South America, Mexico, Southeast Asia, Europe and the United States, where celebrities like Olivia Rodrigo were in attendance.\nFollowing the album’s narrative, the stage is centred around the fictional universe of DRAAG, an authoritarian state in which the band are presented as ‘The Black Parade’, while incorporating mythology surrounding MOAT, a reconditioning facility where the band’s characters were held captive.\nThe production was brought to life with the help of TAIT, a US-based live-event production and engineering company headquartered in Lititz, Pennsylvania, which designed and built various staging, scenic and automated elements for the tour.\nThe company has previously worked with artists such as BTS member J-Hope on his Hope On The Stage tour.\n“For their monumental return to the stage, MCR transformed stadiums into the fictional country of DRAAG – a surreal domain inspired by an old Russian train station and Gerard Way’s offbeat vision.\n“TAIT helped transform each venue into a retro-futurist landscape of punk pageantry and cinematic storytelling,” TAIT wrote on its website.\nAmong the elements TAIT designed and built were a deep red main stage framed by distressed marble portals, a circular B-stage that brought the band closer to the audience, and a mechanical lift that raised the drum riser during performances.\nBeyond the stadium production, MCR have also expanded the album’s anniversary celebrations through Prelude to The End, an animated short film created with visual artist Lauren Tsai, which features the voices of Hideo Kojima, Phoebe Bridgers, and Billie Eilish.\nMalaysian rock band Pop Shuvit will once again open for the American rock band at the upcoming Kuala Lumpur shows.\nThe announcement marks a reunion of sorts, nearly 19 years after Pop Shuvit supported MCR during the latter’s first Malaysian concert at Stadium Merdeka in December 2007.\nPublic transport operator Rapid KL will extend operating hours across selected rail networks on November 19 and 20 to accommodate concertgoers, as it did during The Weeknd’s concerts earlier this month.\nIn a recent social media post, Rapid KL said the final boarding time at Bukit Jalil LRT station on the Sri Petaling Line is 12.30am for direct services towards both Putra Heights and Sentul Timur.\nPassengers requiring transfers must leave the stadium earlier to catch connecting trains.\nThose connecting to the LRT Ampang Line must board at Bukit Jalil by midnight to catch the final connecting service departing Chan Sow Lin at 12.13am.\nTeenagers, I mean, concertgoers relying on the LRT Kelana Jaya Line, KL Monorail or MRT Kajang Line must board at Bukit Jalil by 11.30pm.\nKelana Jaya Line passengers can transfer at Masjid Jamek to catch the final train to Putra Heights at 12.03am or Gombak at 12.17am.\nFor monorail users, the final connecting services from Hang Tuah depart for Titiwangsa at 12.08am and KL Sentral at 12.10am.\nThose travelling on the MRT Kajang Line will need to make two transfers after boarding at Bukit Jalil by 11.30pm.\nTo ease traffic congestion around Bukit Jalil, Rapid KL urged concertgoers to use its Park and Ride facilities linked to the LRT Ampang, LRT Sri Petaling and MRT Putrajaya lines.\nMotorists can access flat parking rates of between RM4.30 and RM5 per entry at official rail-operated parking facilities, provided they use the same Touch ‘n Go card to enter and exit the car park and pay their train fares.\nCommuters can monitor real-time parking availability on concert days through the MyRapid Pulse mobile application.\nSo, dust off your sneakers, strike a violent pose, and come dressed in black. Till then, so long and goodnight.', 'content_status': 'completed', 'content_error': None, 'embedding': '[-0.017449737,0.0066799894,-0.045342546,0.0134266,0.005245177,0.010205197,-0.00277558,-0.06404565,-0.0015178347,0.045649588,0.0070487624,0.03645859,-0.0013598532,0.008596167,-0.023612604,-0.03476554,0.07687278,0.0059710704,0.007306713,-0.036023937,0.027187973,-0.014832955,-0.03962864,-0.013440133,-0.0013438475,-0.004571611,0.00031492804,0.0021168997,0.0047250553,-0.004604539,-0.03527624,-0.037861425,0.009392668,-0.005788976,-0